      <h2>Signs Your Liverpool Boiler Needs Servicing Now</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">Spot any of these? Do not wait for the annual reminder. We can often come the same day.</p>
      <div class="pf-signs" style="margin-top:2rem;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:1rem;">
        <div class="pf-sign" style="background:#f1f5f9;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:3px solid #dc2626;border-radius:10px;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;"><strong style="display:block;color:#1a3a5c;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Unusual noises from the boiler</strong><p style="margin:0;">Banging, kettling, gurgling or whistling sounds point to faults. Often scale build-up, low inhibitor or a worn part. Far cheaper to fix at a service than after a full breakdown.</p></div>
        <div class="pf-sign" style="background:#f1f5f9;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:3px solid #dc2626;border-radius:10px;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;"><strong style="display:block;color:#1a3a5c;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Boiler locking out again and again</strong><p style="margin:0;">A boiler that keeps shutting itself down is finding a fault. Error codes name the cause. A diagnostic visit spots the issue before a full breakdown.</p></div>
        <div class="pf-sign" style="background:#f1f5f9;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:3px solid #dc2626;border-radius:10px;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;"><strong style="display:block;color:#1a3a5c;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Yellow or orange pilot flame</strong><p style="margin:0;">A healthy flame is blue. Yellow or orange means incomplete burning and a possible CO risk. Stop using the boiler now. If you suspect CO, call <a href="https://cadentgas.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cadent Gas on 0800 111 999</a> first, then call us.</p></div>
        <div class="pf-sign" style="background:#f1f5f9;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:3px solid #dc2626;border-radius:10px;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;"><strong style="display:block;color:#1a3a5c;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Boiler pressure keeps dropping</strong><p style="margin:0;">A system that loses pressure again and again has a leak in the heating circuit. Left alone, this damages the pump and heat exchanger. A service finds the source.</p></div>
        <div class="pf-sign" style="background:#f1f5f9;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:3px solid #dc2626;border-radius:10px;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;"><strong style="display:block;color:#1a3a5c;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Hot water but no heating. Or heating but no hot water.</strong><p style="margin:0;">Partial failures often point to a zone valve, diverter valve or thermostat fault. All of these are checked in our standard service.</p></div>
        <div class="pf-sign" style="background:#f1f5f9;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:3px solid #dc2626;border-radius:10px;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;"><strong style="display:block;color:#1a3a5c;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">More than 12 months since the last service</strong><p style="margin:0;">An unserviced boiler is an unchecked boiler. Even if it seems to be working, efficiency drops, inhibitor degrades and faults grow unseen. Yearly service is the only way to know.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#f97316;border-radius:10px;padding:1.5rem;text-align:center;margin-top:2rem;">
